<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guna Yala</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as San Blas, Kuna Yala</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guna Yala, formerly San Blas, is an indigenous territory (comarca) of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guna-people">Guna people</a> along <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a>'s north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast, including some 365 islands. After the Guna Revolution of 1925 against attempts to suppress their customs, the Guna gained autonomy, recognised in law in 1938 and 1953. It is governed by the Guna General Congress and is known for its molas, colourful textile panels.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guna-yala.jpg" alt="Guna Yala" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Olosipu at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kuna_Yala_beach_2003.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Guna Yala is a comarca indígena in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> with its capital at Gaigirgordub. It runs as a narrow strip about 373 kilometres long along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast, bordered by Colón province to the west and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> to the east, and covers about 2,306 square kilometres. An archipelago of 365 islands lies off the coast, of which 50 are inhabited. The area was formerly known as San Blas and later as Kuna Yala, and in October 2011 the government of Panama adopted the present name, accepting the people's claim that Guna is closer to their own word.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>When the Spaniards arrived in the 1600s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guna-people">Kuna</a> lived near the Gulf of Urabá, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>. Contact brought significant violence and limited trade, and they fled towards Darién. They first settled along rivers flowing into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, but from the mid-1800s they began to move to islands near the river mouths, which sheltered them from illness, snakes and mosquitoes and gave access to coastal trade in coconuts and turtle-shell. They kept farms on the mainland. In the late 1690s Scottish colonists founded New Edinburgh on the Bay of Caledonia as part of the Darien scheme; the town surrendered to a Spanish siege in March 1700.</p><p>A Colombian act of 4 June 1870 created the Tulenega Shire and recognised the Guna as owners of the land. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> separated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> in 1903 the act was ignored. Incursions by outsiders seeking gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> and sea turtles, banana concessions and police abuse of the Guna, including suppression of their customs, led to the rebellion of 1925, led by Nele Kantule of Ustupu and Ologintipipilele of Ailigandi.</p><p>The Guna proclaimed the independent Republic of Tule for a few days. A peace agreement was signed on 4 March 1925 in the presence of the American minister John G. South, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> committed itself to protecting Guna customs. Legislation of 1938 created the Guna District of San Blas, and Act No. 16 of 1953 finalised its boundaries and administration.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Under Law 16 of 1953 the highest authority is the General Congress, made up of representatives of all communities, which meets twice a year. The comarca is divided into four corregimientos with 51 communities, most of them on islands. The economy rests on farming for subsistence, fishing, tourism and crafts, and the making of molas is the main activity of Guna women and the chief income of many families. 32,016 people lived there in 2023, more than 95 per cent of them indigenous.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Guna Yala” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Panama is divided into ten provinces and several Indigenous comarcas, such as <b>Guna Yala</b>, which enjoy substantial self-government.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-panama">Republic of Panama · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
